= Lesson 12 — Decode and control

== Fixed fields, decoded in parallel

Every other field's #emph[meaning] depends on the opcode, so the opcode must be
decodable before anything else — which is why it is always `inst[6:0]`. Putting
it at a fixed position means the register fields, `funct3` and the immediate can
all be extracted #strong[in parallel] with decoding the opcode rather than
after it.

The register fields never move either: `rs1` is always `inst[19:15]`, `rs2`
always `inst[24:20]`, `rd` always `inst[11:7]`. So lesson 13 addresses the
register file #emph[before the instruction has been understood at all].

#fig("/figures/rv32i-formats.svg",
     [The six formats. Only the shaded immediate fields are rearranged; every
      other field stays where it is.], width: 100%)

== The immediates look like vandalism

B puts `imm[11]` in `inst[7]` while `imm[12]` is in `inst[31]`. J shuffles its
bits out of order entirely. Read as a table it looks arbitrary.

Two rules explain all of it:

+ #strong[The sign bit is always `inst[31]`.] Every format. So sign extension
  needs no multiplexer at all — just replicate one fixed wire.
+ #strong[Each immediate bit comes from as few different places as possible.]
  `imm[10:5]` is `inst[30:25]` in I, S, B #emph[and] J. `imm[4:1]` is
  `inst[24:21]` in I and J, `inst[11:8]` in S and B.

Rule 2 is #emph[why] B and J look shuffled: bit positions were assigned to stay
aligned #strong[across] formats, not in order #strong[within] one.

#measured("count the multiplexer inputs and the design appears")[
  Of the 32 immediate output bits, counting how many distinct instruction-word
  positions feed each — a bit with #emph[N] sources needs an #emph[N]-input
  multiplexer:
  #ttable(
    columns: (auto, auto),
    [*sources*], [*bits*],
    [1 — no multiplexer at all], [#strong[24]],
    [2], [7],
    [3], [1],
  )
  #strong[41 multiplexer inputs across all 32 bits], against an upper bound of
  160 if the five layouts were uncorrelated — 26% of it.

  And the single bit needing three sources is `imm[11]` — precisely the one
  that looks most arbitrary in the specification. The designers put the
  ugliness in #emph[one place] rather than spreading it everywhere.
]

#why[
  "The encoding is weird" and "the encoding is carefully optimised" turn out to
  be the same observation from two sides. You cannot tell which from reading
  the specification; you can tell immediately from the multiplexer count.
]

Note also that B and J immediates are implicitly multiplied by two — bit 0 is
always zero and is not stored, because instructions are 2-byte aligned. One
extra bit of branch range, free.

== The trap

Lesson 10's ALU takes `{inst[30], funct3}`, which falls straight out of the
instruction word for R-type. For I-type it very nearly does:

```
addi x1, x2, -1
```

has `imm = 0xFFF`, so `inst[31:20]` is all ones and #strong[`inst[30]` is 1].
Pass `{inst[30], funct3} = {1, 000}` to the ALU and it performs #strong[SUB]
instead of ADD — so `addi` with any negative immediate silently subtracts.

`inst[30]` is only meaningful for `OP-IMM` when `funct3 == 101`, distinguishing
`SRLI` from `SRAI`. Everywhere else in `OP-IMM` it is part of the immediate and
must be forced to zero.

#watch[
  This is invisible in an R-type-only test, and any immediate at or beyond 1024
  — or any negative one — trips it. Without the fix, lesson 13's first program
  computes nonsense.
]

#found[
  A bug in the first draft of this decoder, caught by the linter rather than by
  a test: `alu_op` was assigned from `{alu_bit30, funct3}`, and a #emph[separate]
  wire applied the "loads and stores must add" override — and was never
  connected to anything. Every load would have asked the ALU for whatever
  `funct3` happened to mean. One assignment per output.
]

== Testing a decoder

The vectors are #strong[real encodings], generated by a small assembler and
each labelled with the assembly it came from — every format, the sign-extended
extremes of each, and eight malformed instructions that must raise `illegal`.

#strong[The legal contrast cases matter as much as the illegal ones.] A decoder
that rejected everything would pass all the illegal tests, so encodings sitting
right next to them — `blt`, `bge`, `sra` — are asserted #emph[not] to be
flagged.

#watch[
  One of the lesson's own illegal vectors was simply wrong. `0x00314863` was
  written intending "branch with `funct3=011`"; it actually has `funct3=100`,
  which is `blt` and perfectly legal. The decoder correctly declined to flag it
  and the test failed. Hand-computing instruction encodings is error-prone —
  compute them with a script and print the fields back to check.
]

#found[
  Seven mutations; six caught immediately. The survivor forced S-type `imm[0]`
  to zero — and was invisible because #strong[every store offset in the vectors
  happened to be even]. Adding `sb x3,7(x2)` catches it at once.

  Unlike lesson 11's surviving mutation, which was benign because the design
  was redundant, this one was a #emph[real missing test]. Both outcomes are
  useful, and telling them apart is the skill.
]

